BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:An Artist Responds to War: Documentary & Discussion with Mohsen Ma
 hdawi
DTSTART:20260829T160000
DTEND:20260829T173000
DTSTAMP:20260829T154018Z
UID:5a2492a4bf9dd357cbb9a7d1d6887c@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Palestinian Rights Activist Mohsen Mahdawi Joins Discussion at
  Peter Schumann Biopic Screenings St. Johnsbury- Filmmaker Robbie Leppzer 
 will be joined on August 29th by Palestinian Vermonter and human rights ac
 tivist Mohsen Mahdawi for the Catamount Arts St. Johnsbury screenings of\,
  “An Artist Responds to War\,” a vibrant documentary about Peter Schum
 ann\, the visionary founder of the Bread & Puppet Theater. There will be a
  screening at 4pm and a screening at 7pm. Discussions will follow both scr
 eenings. Born in Nazi Germany and raised amidst the terror of aerial bomba
 rdments\, Schumann channeled his childhood trauma into one of the most bou
 ndary-breaking landmark theater companies in the world. Under Schumann’s
  direction\, Bread & Puppet’s six decades of radical political performan
 ce have been forged from a single question Schumann has been exploring sin
 ce youth: how do we as humans respond to the madness of war and violence? 
 Founded by Peter and Elka Schumann in New York City in the early 1960s\, t
 he Bread & Puppet Theater helped to revolutionize street protests against 
 the Vietnam War by staging pageants of gigantic papier maché puppets in p
 ublic. In the 1970s\, Bread & Puppet moved to its current home on a 250-ac
 re Vermont farm where their epic anti-war puppetry\, pageantry\, and spect
 acle continue. Over six decades\, Bread & Puppet has redefined political t
 heater and public art in the United States\, becoming one of the most icon
 ic art groups to emerge from the 20th century. Today\, as international no
 rms collapse and atrocities unfold in plain sight in Gaza\, Peter Schumann
  finds his life’s central question more pressing than ever. At 92 years 
 old\, Schumann continues his relentless work with fierce urgency\, using p
 aintings and epic scale performances to represent to audiences the true co
 st of war. Drawing on originally shot footage and a remarkable archive spa
 nning more than 40 years\, film director Robbie Leppzer offers an intimate
  window into the man\, his art\, and his unbroken faith that creativity ca
 n stand against the brutality of power. In an age desperate for hopeful fi
 gures of cultural resistance\, “An Artist Responds to War\,” is a test
 ament to the enduring radical idea that art is not a retreat from the worl
 d but a way of fighting for it. Following each 45-minute screening of\, 
 “An Artist Reponds to War\,” filmmaker Robbie Leppzer will be in conve
 rsation with Vermont Palestinian human rights advocate Mohsen Mahdawi abou
 t his life growing up in Palestine’s West Bank\, his human rights work\,
  and his ongoing legal battles against the Trump administration’s effort
 s to deport him. Learn more at catamountarts.org.\n\nSources: Vermont Publ
 ic
LOCATION:Catamount Arts\, 115 Eastern Ave.
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/an-artist-
 responds-to-war-documentary-discussion-with-mohsen-mahdawi-25-08-2026-12-1
 1-48
END:VEVENT
