BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reception: Peter Schumann Exhibition at Catamount Arts
DTSTART:20260829T173000
DTEND:20260829T183000
DTSTAMP:20260829T154018Z
UID:2ad392663ca2b8095313266d62f84b@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:At the exhibition’s center are the Isenheim Studies\, 64 mas
 onite prints carved\, in 1962\, by Peter Schumann\, cofounder of Bread & P
 uppet. Echoing themes of bodily pain and collective grief\, the work is re
 framed for the age of drone warfare. Hanging alongside archival anti-war b
 anners from the 1960s are vertical banners of red\, green\, and black popp
 ies\, printed in 2025 for the Bread & Puppet circus\, symbolizing mourning
  and solidarity with Palestine. This exhibition was included at Printed Ma
 tter’s 2025 NYABF at MoMA PS1\, in September of 2025\, and LAABF\, in Ma
 y of 2026. German Renaissance painter Matthias Grünewald (c. 1470–1528)
  created the Isenheim Altarpiece (1512–1516) for the St. Antonite monast
 ery in Colmar\, France\, where monks treated patients afflicted with ergot
 ism\, a disease caused by rye fungus. The altarpiece depicts intense ailme
 nts and suffering\, as well as miraculous healing and resurrection. Grüne
 wald witnessed a total solar eclipse while crossing the Alps\, in 1502\, a
 n experience that shaped his use of extreme colors and is also depicted in
  the Altarpiece. The Isenheim Altarpiece has influenced Bread and Puppet
 ’s Peter Schumann for over sixty years. The 64 Isenheim Studies presente
 d in the current exhibition are the earliest known references to Grünewal
 d in Schumann’s work. In 2024\, the complete set\, which had never been 
 printed\, was discovered in a box labeled\, “Grünewald woodcuts\, early
  1960s\,” in Bread & Puppet cofounder Elka Schumann’s handwriting. Pet
 er Schumann carved the studies as he saw them\, not in reverse\, so the pr
 inted images appear reversed from the Grünewald painting. Accompanying th
 e prints is a text written in 2024 by Peter Schumann in response to the US
 /Israeli genocide of Palestinians in Gaza\, a meditation on war\, empire\,
  and human responsibility. Aptly\, the historical Jesus was a Palestinian 
 Jew persecuted\, tortured\, and executed by the Romans for rebelling again
 st the empire\, a fate shared by many Palestinians today. Born in Nazi Ger
 many and shaped by a childhood riddled with aerial bombardments\, Peter Sc
 humann is the visionary founder of the Bread & Puppet Theater\, an endurin
 g\, iconic troupe of artists and performers who redefined political theate
 r and public art in America. For over six decades\, Schumann’s radical p
 olitical performance has grappled with a single question forged in his chi
 ldhood: how do we as humans respond to the madness of war and violence? Sc
 humann and his wife Elka founded Bread & Puppet in New York City in the ea
 rly 1960s\, revolutionizing anti-war protests with public pageants of enor
 mous papier maché puppet the streets. In the 1970s\, Bread & Puppet moved
  to its current home on a 250-acre Vermont farm where their epic anti-war 
 puppetry\, pageantry\, and spectacle continue today. Before and after the 
 August 29th reception\, Catamount Arts has scheduled two screenings (at 4p
 m and 7pm) of “An Artist Responds to War\,” a 45-minute documentary by
  director Robbie Leppzer. The film offers an intimate examination of Peter
  Schumann’s life\, art\, and relentless faith in human creativity as a t
 ool of resistance. A discussion with Leppzer and well-known Palestinian Ve
 rmonter and human rights advocate Moshen Mahdawi will follow each screenin
 g. Please note that the gallery reception is free\, but admission to the f
 ilm is ticketed. The Fried Family Gallery and Rankin Gallery at Catamount 
 Arts are dedicated to an exploration of contemporary art in curated exhibi
 tions that both challenge and delight viewers. Each year the galleries pre
 sent work by member artists in the acclaimed Arts Connect at Catamount Art
 s Juried Show. Learn more at catamountarts.org.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Catamount Arts\, 115 Eastern Ave.
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/reception-
 peter-schumann-exhibition-at-catamount-arts-25-08-2026-12-01-40
END:VEVENT
